==== stdout ====
raphael help addressing - Paths, ids, slugs, containment and field limits.

Paths are absolute: /work/raphael/backend. The root is /. For commands that address one item, use a path or --id, never both. List and search may mix paths and repeated --id. The root can be listed and searched, but cannot be read or changed as an item. A path changes when an item or an ancestor moves or changes slug; its positive integer id stays the same. --id is repeatable only on list and search.

Types: area, project, resource.note. Items that can hold children: area, project.

The root (/) can directly hold area.
area can directly hold area, project, resource.note.
project can directly hold resource.note.
resource.note cannot hold children.

NFC lowercase Unicode letter or number tokens joined by single hyphens; combining marks may follow the first letter or number in each token. A slug may contain at most 100 Unicode characters.

Create with a full path, or with --parent-id and --slug. The final path segment is the new slug. Parent items must already exist. Renaming a title does not change the slug; use update --slug to change its address. Archive retains the item and reserves its slug. There is no delete command.

Titles are required and may contain at most 200 characters after trimming. Descriptions may contain at most 4000 characters. Give each tag its own --tag: 1–50 characters per tag, at most 25 tags. Tags are trimmed and NFC-normalized, are case-sensitive, and must not repeat or contain commas. Metadata is a JSON object set once at create: at most 50 top-level keys, 5 levels deep, 100 characters per key, and 16384 serialized UTF-8 bytes.

The default page contains at most 50 results. --limit accepts 1–500; --skip skips results. At most 10 paths or ids may be searched or listed in one request.

For list: --order-by accepts slug, updatedAt, id with asc or desc; for example --order-by updatedAt:desc. Search orders by relevance and does not accept --order-by.

--filter accepts a JSON object with keys type, kind, tags. Allowed type values: area, project, resource. Allowed kind values: note. Each value is a scalar or {"$in":[...]}; sibling keys are ANDed. Tags match at least one supplied tag, with exact case after trimming and NFC normalization. A kind does not imply a type.

Examples:
  raphael get /work/raphael
  raphael get --id 42
  raphael create project --parent-id 42 --slug raphael --title Raphael

Next: raphael help reference
